-- Q10 [Male Hormonal Axis | hard | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub10$Male Hormonal Axis$sub10$, $qt10$A man undergoing fertility evaluation receives a recombinant inhibin B preparation that raises his circulating inhibin to the high end of the physiological range while every other input to his hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis is held constant. A physiologist measures his pituitary gonadotropins and Leydig-cell output shortly afterward.$qt10$, $op10$[{"label": "A", "text": "FSH and LH both fall, because the elevated inhibin suppresses the pituitary's release of every gonadotropin in parallel"}, {"label": "B", "text": "LH falls while FSH is preserved, because the elevated inhibin feeds back specifically onto the LH-driven Leydig-cell arm of the axis"}, {"label": "C", "text": "FSH falls while LH and testosterone are largely unchanged, because the elevated inhibin feeds back specifically to inhibit FSH release"}, {"label": "D", "text": "FSH falls and testosterone rises, because the drop in FSH relieves the Sertoli cells and lets the Leydig cells secrete more testosterone"}]$op10$::jsonb, 'C', $ex10$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is C because inhibin from the Sertoli cells feeds back selectively to inhibit FSH release from the pituitary, leaving the LH-Leydig-testosterone arm largely intact. The text states that "FSH also stimulates the Sertoli cells to produce hormones called inhibins, which function to inhibit FSH release from the pituitary," establishing that inhibin's target is FSH specifically. The LH arm is governed separately: "LH binds to receptors on Leydig cells in the testes and upregulates the production of testosterone," and it is testosterone—not inhibin—that "binds to androgen receptors on both the hypothalamus and the anterior pituitary, inhibiting the synthesis and secretion of GnRH and LH." Because the experiment raises only inhibin while holding other inputs constant, FSH drops selectively while LH-driven testosterone secretion continues largely unchanged. (Choice A) is wrong because inhibin does not suppress every gonadotropin in parallel—that parallel, GnRH-level suppression is the role of testosterone, not inhibin. (Choice B) is wrong because it reverses the arm inhibin targets; inhibin acts on FSH, not on the LH-Leydig arm. (Choice C) is correct because inhibin's negative feedback is selective for FSH, sparing the LH-testosterone loop. (Choice D) is wrong because, although FSH does fall selectively, the Sertoli/FSH arm does not gate Leydig-cell testosterone, which is driven by LH, so testosterone does not rise. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the differential outcome of an experimental perturbation by distinguishing two parallel feedback arms and tracing which gonadotropin and steroid each one controls.$ex10$, 'hard', '3B', $cc10$Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)$cc10$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
