-- Q15 [Female Hormones: Estrogen & Progesterone | hard | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub15$Female Hormones: Estrogen & Progesterone$sub15$, $qt15$A blastocyst successfully implants in the endometrium near the end of the luteal phase, the point at which the corpus luteum would normally begin to degrade. A researcher tracks the endometrial lining over the following two weeks and finds it is maintained rather than shed. Which mechanism best accounts for this outcome?$qt15$, $op15$[{"label": "A", "text": "Because the corpus luteum persists on its own and the lining is shed only when estrogen falls, the endometrium is retained even though no hormone is released by the implanting blastocyst."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The chorion releases hCG, which acts directly on the endometrial lining to maintain it while the corpus luteum degrades and progesterone output falls as usual."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The chorion releases hCG, which keeps the corpus luteum active so that progesterone stays high, maintaining the endometrium rather than allowing it to be shed."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The chorion releases hCG, which stimulates the anterior pituitary to keep secreting FSH and LH, and this sustained gonadotropin output prolongs the corpus luteum and its progesterone."}]$op15$::jsonb, 'C', $ex15$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is C because hCG from the implanting blastocyst's chorion keeps the corpus luteum active, and the resulting sustained progesterone is what maintains the endometrium so it is not shed. Normally, "the corpus luteum degenerates and the levels of estrogen and progesterone decrease" and "the endometrium begins to degenerate as the progesterone levels drop," which initiates menstruation. When implantation occurs, the chorion "begins releasing a hormone called human beta chorionic gonadotropin (β-HCG) which makes its way to the corpus luteum and keeps that structure active. This ensures adequate levels of progesterone that will maintain the endometrium." Thus the rescue chain runs blastocyst chorion to hCG to corpus luteum to progesterone to maintained endometrium, exactly the sequence the researcher observes. (Choice A) is wrong because shedding is triggered by the drop in progesterone from the degenerating corpus luteum, not by falling estrogen, and the corpus luteum does not persist on its own — it requires the hCG signal. (Choice B) is wrong because hCG does not act directly on the uterine lining; it acts on the corpus luteum to sustain progesterone, which in turn maintains the lining. (Choice C) is correct because hCG keeps the corpus luteum active, sustaining the high progesterone that preserves the endometrium. (Choice D) is wrong because hCG itself substitutes for LH's support of the corpus luteum rather than working by driving the anterior pituitary to release FSH and LH. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the endocrine fate of the endometrium by tracing a multi-step hormonal feedback chain from an implantation scenario to the maintained-lining outcome.$ex15$, 'hard', '3B', $cc15$Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)$cc15$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
