-- Q16 [The Menstrual Cycle | easy | Skill 1 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub16$The Menstrual Cycle$sub16$, $qt16$During the early follicular phase of a healthy patient's ovarian cycle, a reproductive endocrinologist tracks a cohort of five or six tertiary follicles and observes them expand in diameter as they grow on the surface of the ovary. Which anterior pituitary gonadotropin most directly stimulates this growth and expansion of the tertiary follicles?$qt16$, $op16$[{"label": "A", "text": "Progesterone, secreted by the corpus luteum, which drives the early tertiary follicles to enlarge"}, {"label": "B", "text": "LH (luteinizing hormone), which stimulates the granulosa and theca cells to secrete estradiol"}, {"label": "C", "text": "GnRH from the hypothalamus, which acts directly on the ovarian follicles to make them grow"}, {"label": "D", "text": "FSH (follicle-stimulating hormone), which stimulates the tertiary follicles to grow and expand in diameter"}]$op16$::jsonb, 'D', $ex16$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is D because follicle-stimulating hormone is the anterior pituitary gonadotropin that directly drives follicular growth during the follicular phase. The text states that FSH and LH bind to receptors on the granulosa and theca cells of the follicles, and that "FSH stimulates the follicles to grow (hence its name of follicle-stimulating hormone), and the five or six tertiary follicles expand in diameter." LH, by contrast, "stimulates the granulosa and theca cells of the follicles to produce the sex steroid hormone estradiol," a separate action from the growth/expansion step the scenario asks about. The growth and diameter expansion of the tertiary follicles is therefore the defining action of FSH. (Choice A) Progesterone is not a pituitary gonadotropin and is produced by the corpus luteum during the luteal phase, where it "keeps GnRH, LH, and FSH secretions low, so no new dominant follicles develop," making it a suppressor rather than a driver of follicle growth. (Choice B) LH does act on the same follicles, but it stimulates the granulosa and theca cells "to produce the sex steroid hormone estradiol," not the follicle growth and diameter expansion named in the scenario. (Choice C) GnRH comes from the hypothalamus, but its receptors are on the anterior pituitary, where its "binding ... stimulates release of the two gonadotropins," so it acts on the pituitary rather than directly on the ovarian follicles. (Choice D) FSH is correct because the text explicitly states it stimulates the follicles to grow and causes the tertiary follicles to expand in diameter. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the specific function of FSH in directly stimulating tertiary follicle growth during the follicular phase.$ex16$, 'easy', '3B', $cc16$Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)$cc16$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a16A$Casting a luteal-phase suppressor hormone (progesterone) as the driver of the process it actually inhibits$a16A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a16B$Naming a real gonadotropin that acts on the same follicles but performs a different named action (estradiol production, not follicle growth)$a16B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a16C$Placing the upstream hypothalamic releasing hormone's target on the ovary instead of the anterior pituitary$a16C$ FROM q;

-- Q17 [The Menstrual Cycle | easy | Skill 1 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub17$The Menstrual Cycle$sub17$, $qt17$After ovulation, the surge of luteinizing hormone transforms the collapsed ovarian follicle into a corpus luteum, whose luteinized granulosa and theca cells now release large amounts of a single steroid that keeps the thickened stratum functionalis intact. A physiologist asks which hormone the corpus luteum secretes in large amounts during this luteal phase to maintain the endometrium.$qt17$, $op17$[{"label": "A", "text": "Progesterone, which the luteinized cells of the corpus luteum produce in large amounts to maintain the stratum functionalis"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Estradiol, which the luteinized cells of the corpus luteum now produce in place of progesterone to maintain the stratum functionalis"}, {"label": "C", "text": "FSH, which the luteinized cells of the corpus luteum produce in large amounts to maintain the stratum functionalis"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Inhibin, which the luteinized cells of the corpus luteum produce in large amounts to maintain the stratum functionalis"}]$op17$::jsonb, 'A', $ex17$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is A because, after luteinization, the corpus luteum's granulosa and theca cells switch to producing large amounts of progesterone, the steroid that sustains the endometrial lining. The text states that "Instead of estrogen, the luteinized granulosa and theca cells of the corpus luteum begin to produce large amounts of the sex steroid hormone progesterone" and that "The post-ovulatory increase in progesterone, which characterizes the luteal phase, is key for maintaining a thick stratum functionalis." As long as a functional corpus luteum secretes progesterone, the endometrial lining stays prepared for implantation; when progesterone production ceases, the lining is shed. (Choice A) is correct because progesterone is the dominant steroid the corpus luteum makes after ovulation to maintain the stratum functionalis. (Choice B) is wrong because estrogen is secreted earlier by the growing follicle; after luteinization the cells produce progesterone "instead of estrogen," so estradiol is not the hormone now made in large amounts. (Choice C) is wrong because FSH is a gonadotropin secreted by the anterior pituitary, not an ovarian steroid, and progesterone's negative feedback keeps FSH low during the luteal phase. (Choice D) is wrong because inhibin, though a real ovarian hormone from granulosa cells, "inhibits the secretion of FSH" rather than maintaining the endometrium. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the established fact that the corpus luteum secretes progesterone to maintain the endometrium during the luteal phase.$ex17$, 'easy', '3B', $cc17$Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)$cc17$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
