UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a17D$Real ovarian hormone with the wrong function: inhibin is secreted by granulosa cells but inhibits FSH rather than maintaining the endometrium.$a17D$ FROM q;

-- Q18 [The Menstrual Cycle | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub18$The Menstrual Cycle$sub18$, $qt18$A patient's oocyte is not fertilized during a given cycle, so no implantation signal reaches the corpus luteum, which then regresses into the corpus albicans. A clinician asks a student to predict the specific hormonal event that directly triggers the shedding of the stratum functionalis as menses begins.$qt18$, $op18$[{"label": "A", "text": "A surge of progesterone released by the regressing corpus luteum drives the lining to slough off and bleed"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A fall in progesterone caused by corpus luteum regression withdraws hormonal support from the lining, triggering its breakdown"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A rise in estrogen from a newly dominant follicle dissolves the lining and brings on menstrual flow"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A second LH surge ruptures the endometrial spiral vessels and forces the lining to detach"}]$op18$::jsonb, 'B', $ex18$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is B because once no implantation occurs, the corpus luteum degrades and progesterone concentrations fall, and it is this withdrawal of progesterone support that triggers the stratum functionalis to break down and shed as menses. The text states that "progesterone concentrations decline as a result of the degradation of the corpus luteum... This decline in progesterone triggers the shedding of the stratum functionalis of the endometrium," and Biology 2e confirms that "the endometrium begins to degenerate as the progesterone levels drop." The trigger is therefore the loss, not the presence, of progesterone, and it is distinct from the upstream events of follicular estrogen secretion and the ovulatory LH surge. The student must chain corpus luteum regression to the progesterone decline and then to endometrial shedding to identify the correct mechanism. (Choice A) is reversed: a regressing corpus luteum cannot surge progesterone, and it is the fall, not a rise, of progesterone that initiates shedding. (Choice B) is correct because the withdrawal of progesterone support after corpus luteum regression is the direct trigger for breakdown of the stratum functionalis. (Choice C) confuses phases, since rising estrogen from a developing follicle rebuilds the endometrium during the proliferative phase rather than causing it to shed. (Choice D) reflects a misconception, because the LH surge triggers ovulation around day 14, whereas menses follows the later progesterone withdrawal at the end of the luteal phase. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because the student must take a described scenario of failed implantation and reason through the corpus luteum regression and progesterone decline to predict the specific hormonal event that causes the lining to shed.$ex18$, 'medium', '3B', $cc18$Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)$cc18$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a18A$Treats a hormone surge rather than its withdrawal as the trigger for a downstream event$a18A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a18C$Assigns a later-phase rebuilding hormone (estrogen) to the shedding step it actually precedes$a18C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a18D$Attributes menstruation to the ovulatory LH surge instead of progesterone withdrawal$a18D$ FROM q;

-- Q19 [The Menstrual Cycle | medium | Skill 2 | ans C]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub19$The Menstrual Cycle$sub19$, $qt19$During the late follicular phase, a single dominant follicle secretes estrogen at concentrations far higher than the entire cohort of follicles produced earlier, and a clinician records a sudden, large spike in luteinizing hormone (LH) immediately afterward. What change at the anterior pituitary best accounts for this LH surge?$qt19$, $op19$[{"label": "A", "text": "The pituitary remains under negative feedback, so the elevated estrogen drives LH secretion down to its lowest level of the cycle"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The elevated estrogen acts directly on the ovary to release the LH surge, bypassing the pituitary entirely"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The pituitary switches from negative to positive feedback, so the elevated estrogen now triggers a large burst of LH secretion"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Rising progesterone crosses the threshold that flips the pituitary to positive feedback, triggering the LH burst"}]$op19$::jsonb, 'C', $ex19$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is C because at the extremely high estrogen concentrations produced by the dominant follicle, the anterior pituitary's response to estrogen reverses from negative to positive feedback, so more estrogen now triggers a large burst of LH rather than suppressing it. Earlier in the follicular phase, rising estrogen follows a classic negative feedback loop that reduces GnRH, LH, and FSH; but once only the dominant follicle remains, it "produces so much estrogen that the normal negative feedback doesn't occur. Instead, these extremely high concentrations of systemic plasma estrogen trigger a regulatory switch in the anterior pituitary that responds by secreting large amounts of LH and FSH" (A&P 2e). It is this large burst of LH, the LH surge, that leads to ovulation of the dominant follicle. The text explicitly notes that "the positive feedback loop by which more estrogen triggers release of more LH and FSH only occurs at this point in the cycle." (Choice A) is a partial truth: estrogen does exert negative feedback at the moderate concentrations seen earlier in the follicular phase, but at the extremely high concentrations from the dominant follicle the relationship reverses to positive feedback, so LH rises rather than falling to its lowest level. (Choice B) misplaces the regulatory event: the switch occurs in the anterior pituitary, which is the structure that actually secretes the LH, not in the ovary itself. (Choice C) is correct because the high-estrogen signal flips the pituitary from negative to positive feedback, producing the LH surge. (Choice D) confuses the timing of the steroids: progesterone dominates the later luteal phase, where it triggers negative feedback to keep LH and FSH low, whereas it is high estrogen from the dominant follicle that flips the pituitary to positive feedback and drives the surge. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting how the direction of an endocrine feedback relationship reverses once a quantitative threshold of estrogen is crossed, rather than recalling a single isolated fact.$ex19$, 'medium', '3B', $cc19$Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)$cc19$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
