SELECT id, 'A', 'partial_truth', $a19A$Correct for moderate estrogen levels but ignores the reversal to positive feedback at high concentrations$a19A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a19B$Locates the regulatory switch in the wrong organ (ovary instead of anterior pituitary)$a19B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a19D$Swaps the luteal-phase steroid (progesterone) for the follicular-phase trigger (estrogen)$a19D$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [The Menstrual Cycle | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub20$The Menstrual Cycle$sub20$, $qt20$In a controlled experiment, researchers surgically remove the corpus luteum from a non-pregnant participant on day 22 of a 28-day cycle, while leaving the ovaries and a cohort of small antral follicles intact. Compared with an untreated control cycle, the investigators predict that the participant's reproductive hormones and follicles will respond by$qt20$, $op20$[{"label": "A", "text": "keeping GnRH, FSH, and LH suppressed and ovulation blocked, because estradiol from the remaining follicles sustains the negative feedback the corpus luteum had supplied"}, {"label": "B", "text": "firing a second LH surge that immediately re-ovulates the original dominant follicle, because losing progesterone removes the brake on positive feedback"}, {"label": "C", "text": "raising estrogen and progesterone together to thicken the endometrium further and delay menses, because the pituitary compensates by boosting both ovarian steroids"}, {"label": "D", "text": "raising GnRH, FSH, and LH so a new cohort of tertiary follicles grows and secretes estrogen, restarting the follicular phase early"}]$op20$::jsonb, 'D', $ex20$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is D because the corpus luteum is the source of luteal-phase progesterone, and progesterone's negative feedback is what holds GnRH, FSH, and LH low so that no new dominant follicle matures; removing the gland mid-luteal phase eliminates that progesterone, releasing the suppression. With the brake gone, the Anatomy and Physiology text states that "During this time of reduced progesterone secretion, FSH and LH are once again stimulated, and the follicular phase begins again with a new cohort of early tertiary follicles beginning to grow and secrete estrogen" (lines 56504-56505), which Biology 2e corroborates: "The decrease in progesterone also allows the hypothalamus to send GnRH to the anterior pituitary, releasing FSH and LH and starting the cycles again" (lines 57373-57374). Surgically removing the corpus luteum on day 22 simply forces this drop early, so the follicular phase restarts ahead of schedule. (Choice A) is a partial truth, since estradiol can exert negative feedback, but with the corpus luteum gone progesterone falls and "FSH and LH are once again stimulated" (line 56504), so gonadotropins rise rather than stay suppressed. (Choice B) confuses process steps: the LH surge requires the high-estrogen positive-feedback switch that "only occurs at this point in the cycle" around ovulation (line 56473), and the ovulated follicle is already luteinized and its oocyte expelled, so merely losing progesterone cannot re-ovulate it. (Choice C) reverses the relationship, because removing the progesterone source lowers progesterone, and "The endometrium begins to degenerate as the progesterone levels drop, initiating the next menstrual cycle" (lines 57372-57373), so the lining breaks down rather than thickening. (Choice D) is correct because eliminating the corpus luteum removes progesterone's negative feedback, disinhibiting GnRH, FSH, and LH and triggering a fresh wave of estrogen-secreting tertiary follicles. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the outcome of an experimental manipulation by tracing a multi-step negative-feedback chain from a removed gland to downstream gonadotropin and follicle behavior.$ex20$, 'hard', '3B', $cc20$Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)$cc20$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
