UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a23B$Treating the hormone-responsive target tissue (stratum functionalis) as an autonomous endocrine source of the steroids it merely responds to$a23B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a23D$Inventing recruitment of a new follicle/second corpus luteum during pregnancy, when gonadotropin suppression prevents new follicle development$a23D$ FROM q;

-- Q24 [Pregnancy & Menopause | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub24$Pregnancy & Menopause$sub24$, $qt24$A 52-year-old patient who has not menstruated in over a year has blood drawn, and the lab reports markedly elevated FSH and LH alongside very low estrogen. A student is puzzled that the gonadotropins are high even though the ovaries have essentially stopped functioning. Which mechanism best accounts for this hormonal pattern?$qt24$, $op24$[{"label": "A", "text": "The aging pituitary becomes hypersensitive to GnRH and oversecretes FSH and LH, which in turn drives estrogen to abnormally high levels"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Rising estrogen from atretic follicles exerts positive feedback on the pituitary, pushing FSH and LH steadily upward"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The hypothalamus stops releasing GnRH, so FSH and LH rise because the pituitary is no longer restrained by hypothalamic input"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The ovaries lose sensitivity to FSH and LH and exhaust their follicles, so estrogen falls and its negative feedback on the pituitary is lifted, allowing FSH and LH to climb"}]$op24$::jsonb, 'D', $ex24$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is D because menopause originates at the ovary: the ovaries become progressively less sensitive to gonadotropins and their follicles are depleted by atresia, so estrogen production falls off dramatically. Reproductive hormones normally exert negative feedback on the hypothalamic-pituitary axis (\"high levels of reproductive hormones inhibit the release of GnRH\"), so when ovarian estrogen collapses, that inhibition is removed and the pituitary secretes FSH and LH at high levels. The high gonadotropins cannot rescue function because the follicles are gone and unresponsive, which is exactly why the gonadotropins stay elevated rather than being suppressed. (Choice A) is wrong because the rise in gonadotropins is driven by lost ovarian feedback, not by pituitary hypersensitivity, and estrogen falls dramatically rather than rising. (Choice B) reverses the feedback sign: the ovarian steroids act through negative, not positive, feedback, and atretic follicles are dying rather than secreting more estrogen. (Choice C) misplaces the lesion at the hypothalamus, but GnRH is still released; removing GnRH would lower, not raise, FSH and LH because GnRH is the stimulus that drives gonadotropin secretion. (Choice D) correctly traces the loss of ovarian responsiveness and follicle depletion to falling estrogen, removed negative feedback, and the resulting climb in FSH and LH. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires integrating the direction of the gonadotropin-estrogen feedback loop with the site of ovarian failure to predict why the gonadotropins rise rather than fall.$ex24$, 'hard', '3B', $cc24$Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)$cc24$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a24A$Blames an aging pituitary that oversecretes and pushes estrogen up, the opposite of the actual estrogen collapse$a24A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a24B$Flips the feedback sign to positive and treats dying atretic follicles as estrogen sources$a24B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a24C$Relocates the failure to the hypothalamus and confuses removing GnRH with raising gonadotropins$a24C$ FROM q;

-- Q25 [Pregnancy & Menopause | hard | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub25$Pregnancy & Menopause$sub25$, $qt25$In a successful early pregnancy, serum progesterone remains elevated well beyond the 10-to-12-day point at which it normally collapses in a non-fertilized cycle. A researcher comparing the two luteal phases concludes that the difference reflects a single hormonal signal originating from the conceptus. Which mechanism accounts for the sustained progesterone in the pregnant cycle?$qt25$, $op25$[{"label": "A", "text": "A sustained, non-transient LH surge from the pituitary keeps the corpus luteum secreting progesterone past the usual window"}, {"label": "B", "text": "hCG from the implanting embryo directs the corpus luteum to persist and keep secreting progesterone rather than degrading into the corpus albicans"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Rising FSH recruits a dominant follicle to luteinize a second time, supplying a fresh source of progesterone past the usual window"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Implantation directly stimulates the endometrial spiral arteries to secrete progesterone, bypassing the corpus luteum past the usual window"}]$op25$::jsonb, 'B', $ex25$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is B because the embryonic trophoblast secretes human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), which "directs the corpus luteum to survive, enlarge, and continue producing progesterone," so the corpus luteum does not regress on its usual schedule. In a non-fertilized cycle, "if pregnancy does not occur within 10 to 12 days, the corpus luteum will stop secreting progesterone and degrade into the corpus albicans," and progesterone falls; the only thing that changes that default in early pregnancy is the rescue signal from the conceptus. The text states explicitly that "if an embryo implants, signals are sent to the corpus luteum to continue secreting progesterone... If an embryo does not implant, no signal is sent to the corpus luteum and it degrades." Thus the single conceptus-derived variable that sustains progesterone is hCG rescuing the corpus luteum. (Choice A) is wrong because the LH surge is the transient trigger of luteinization at ovulation, not a sustained pregnancy signal; progesterone-driven negative feedback actually keeps LH low during the luteal phase. (Choice B) is correct because hCG is the embryonic signal that maintains the corpus luteum and its progesterone output past the normal 10-to-12-day window. (Choice C) is wrong because luteal-phase progesterone suppresses FSH and LH so no new dominant follicle develops; FSH only rises again once the corpus luteum has already degraded. (Choice D) is wrong because without progesterone the spiral arteries constrict and rupture during menses—they are targets of progesterone withdrawal, not a progesterone source. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires contrasting two physiological scenarios and isolating the single causal variable (the hCG rescue signal) that explains the divergent progesterone trajectories rather than recalling one fact.$ex25$, 'hard', '3B', $cc25$Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)$cc25$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
